Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library uses stream operators (`<<` for writing, `>>` for reading) and that objects with a `serialise()` method can work with these streams bidirectionally.

Based on the test examples, particularly the `experimental_serialise` test which shows objects being serialized and deserialized using their `serialise()` method, here's the completed implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

struct UserData {
    uint32_t user_id;
    std::string name;

    void serialise(auto& stream) {
        stream << user_id << name;
    }
};

void serialize_object(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const UserData& data) {
    // Create a non-const copy to call the serialise method
    UserData temp = data;
    temp.serialise(writer);
}

void deserialize_object(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader, UserData& data) {
    // The serialise method works for both read and write operations
    data.serialise(reader);
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`serialize_object`**: Since the `serialise()` method is defined with `auto& stream`, it works with both readers and writers. For serialization with a writer, we create a temporary copy of the const data and call its `serialise()` method which will use the `<<` operator to write to the stream.

2. **`deserialize_object`**: For deserialization, we directly call the `serialise()` method on the data object with the reader. The same `serialise()` method works bidirectionally - when used with a reader, the `<<` operator acts as a read operation (extracting data from the stream into the variables).

The key insight from the test code is that Hexi's design allows the same `serialise()` method to work for both serialization and deserialization by adapting the stream operators based on whether the stream is a reader or writer.

