Looking at the code fragments, I can see that the Hexi library provides `binary_stream_reader` and `binary_stream_writer` classes with support for reading/writing containers using the `prefixed<T>` adaptor. The `prefixed` adaptor writes a 32-bit count prefix before the container elements.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

void write_container_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::vector<uint32_t>& container) {
    // Write the container with a prefixed count (32-bit little-endian count followed by elements)
    writer << hexi::prefixed(container);
}

std::vector<uint32_t> read_container_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    // Read the container (first reads the 32-bit count, then the elements)
    std::vector<uint32_t> container;
    reader >> hexi::prefixed(container);
    return container;
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`write_container_to_stream`**: Uses the `hexi::prefixed()` adaptor which wraps the container. When used with the `<<` operator, it:
   - Writes a 32-bit count (in little-endian format) representing the number of elements
   - Writes all the elements in the container

2. **`read_container_from_stream`**: Uses the `hexi::prefixed()` adaptor which wraps the container. When used with the `>>` operator, it:
   - Reads a 32-bit count (in little-endian format)
   - Reads that many elements from the stream into the container
   - The container is automatically resized/cleared as needed

The `prefixed` adaptor is the standard way in Hexi to serialize containers with a length prefix, which matches the requirement to write/read containers with a prefixed count.

