Looking at the code fragments, I can see how the Hexi library handles varint encoding/decoding. Based on the library's patterns and the `varint_decode` function references in the code, I'll implement the three functions:

```cpp
#include <hexi/shared.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

using namespace hexi::detail;

template<typename T>
std::size_t encode_varint(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, T value) {
    std::size_t bytes_written = 0;

    // Convert to unsigned for bitwise operations
    using unsigned_type = std::make_unsigned_t<T>;
    unsigned_type uvalue = static_cast<unsigned_type>(value);

    // Encode in groups of 7 bits
    while (uvalue >= 0x80) {
        // Write lower 7 bits with continuation bit set
        std::uint8_t byte = static_cast<std::uint8_t>((uvalue & 0x7F) | 0x80);
        writer << byte;
        uvalue >>= 7;
        bytes_written++;
    }

    // Write final byte (no continuation bit)
    std::uint8_t byte = static_cast<std::uint8_t>(uvalue & 0x7F);
    writer << byte;
    bytes_written++;

    return bytes_written;
}

template<typename T>
T decode_varint(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    using unsigned_type = std::make_unsigned_t<T>;
    unsigned_type result = 0;
    int shift = 0;

    while (true) {
        std::uint8_t byte = 0;
        reader >> byte;

        // Add the lower 7 bits to result
        result |= static_cast<unsigned_type>(byte & 0x7F) << shift;

        // Check if this is the last byte (continuation bit not set)
        if ((byte & 0x80) == 0) {
            break;
        }

        shift += 7;

        // Safety check to prevent infinite loop
        if (shift >= static_cast<int>(sizeof(T) * 8)) {
            break;
        }
    }

    return static_cast<T>(result);
}

template<typename T>
bool encode_decode_roundtrip(T original_value) {
    // Create a buffer for writing
    std::vector<std::uint8_t> buffer;
    hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor write_adaptor(buffer, hexi::init_empty);
    hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer writer(write_adaptor);

    // Encode the value
    encode_varint(writer, original_value);

    // Create a reader from the same buffer
    hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor read_adaptor(buffer);
    hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader reader(read_adaptor);

    // Decode the value
    T decoded_value = decode_varint<T>(reader);

    // Compare original and decoded values
    return original_value == decoded_value;
}
```

This implementation:

1. **`encode_varint`**: Encodes an integer using variable-length encoding where:
   - Each byte stores 7 bits of data
   - The MSB (bit 7) is a continuation bit (1 = more bytes follow, 0 = last byte)
   - Returns the number of bytes written

2. **`decode_varint`**: Decodes a varint-encoded integer from a stream by:
   - Reading bytes one at a time
   - Extracting the lower 7 bits from each byte
   - Shifting and combining them to reconstruct the original value
   - Stopping when a byte with continuation bit = 0 is encountered

3. **`encode_decode_roundtrip`**: Tests the encode/decode cycle by:
   - Creating a buffer and writer to encode the value
   - Creating a reader from the same buffer to decode it
   - Comparing the original and decoded values for equality

The implementation follows the Hexi library's patterns of using `buffer_adaptor` with `binary_stream_writer` and `binary_stream_reader`, and uses the streaming operators (`<<` and `>>`) for I/O operations.

