Based on this pattern and the requirements, here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/shared.h>
#include <array>
#include <cstdint>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace hexi::detail;

template<std::size_t Size>
constexpr auto create_filled_array(std::uint8_t value) {
    std::array<std::uint8_t, Size> target{};
    std::ranges::fill(target, value);
    return target;
}

bool verify_filled_array(const auto& arr, std::uint8_t expected_value) {
    for (const auto& element : arr) {
        if (element != expected_value) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`create_filled_array`**: This is a `constexpr` template function that:
   - Takes a template parameter `Size` to determine the array size at compile time
   - Takes a `value` parameter to fill the array with
   - Creates a zero-initialized `std::array<std::uint8_t, Size>`
   - Uses `std::ranges::fill` to fill all elements with the specified value
   - Returns the filled array
   - This mirrors the pattern shown in the `generate_filled` function from `hexi/shared.h`

2. **`verify_filled_array`**: This function:
   - Takes a generic array parameter using `auto&` (works with any array-like container)
   - Takes an `expected_value` to compare against
   - Iterates through all elements in the array
   - Returns `false` if any element doesn't match the expected value
   - Returns `true` if all elements match

The implementation follows the compile-time constant generation pattern used in the Hexi library and provides runtime verification capability.

